  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 201 PM MDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nGEFS, EC, and LREF ensembles support a 500-mb high moving in \noverhead beginning Friday morning, and remaining through the end of \nthe forecast period. Southerly flow near the surface is favored \nunderneath the ridge. The exception to this would be if shortwave \ntroughs coming over the Rockies could develop a surface low over or \njust to the south of the forecast region, favoring northerly surface \nflow. Otherwise, hot conditions supported by the southerly flow and \nsolar heating are expected for the full duration of the long term \nperiod, with highs forecast in the 90s Friday through Monday. \n\nDry conditions are also forecast in the long term due to the higher \ntemperatures. Relative humidities (RH) look to be in the low to mid \n20s across portions of East-Central Colorado Friday through Monday. \nIt is possible that RH values could drop even further into the teens \nacross portions of the forecast region, with some areas showing as \nhigh as a 40% chance of doing so between Friday and Sunday according \nto LREF guidance. The broadest area covered by these elevated \nprobabilities is on Sunday along and south of I-70, where nearly all \nareas have a 1 in 3 chance or higher of RH values dropping into the \nteens. These lower RH values would be most probable if a shortwave \ntrough could traverse areas north and east of the forecast area \nalong the leading edge of the upper-level high. This could support \nsouthwesterly to westerly flow developing across the forecast area, \nand thus, promote even higher temperatures and drier conditions with \nRH in the teens. Even so, fire weather is not expected during this \nperiod, as wind gusts are forecast to remain below 25 mph in areas \nof the lowest RH values. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms are possible in the long term period, \nhowever NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance shows a 25% chance or less \nacross the forecast region that greater than 0.1 inches of rain will \nbe experienced from Friday to Sunday. These probabilities increase a \nlittle bit across Eastern Colorado Monday, where there is up to a \n45% chance. If storms are able to develop, the most likely area \nwhere they would become severe is across Red Willow County in \nNebraska, and Decatur, Norton, and Graham Counties in Kansas. LREF \nguidance is suggestive of a 40% chance or higher across this zone \nthrough the long term period that sfc-500mb wind shear would \nexceed 30 kts. Even so, uncertainty regarding whether storms \nwould form in addition to their coverage leaves confidence in \nsevere storms occurring less than 5% Friday through Monday.",
